  "CaseNote": [
    {
      "note_id": "NOTE-G2-019-001",
      "case_id": "G2-019",
      "note_type": "admission",
      "note_text": "The longitudinal extract includes warfarin (Warfarin Sodium 5 MG Oral Tablet) and an anticoagulation indication among the active conditions. Other products: Warfarin Sodium 5 MG Oral Tablet. A ground-level fall was followed immediately by right hip pain and inability to bear weight. That was not the prior mobility. fall with postoperative oozing. No chest pain or syncope was described. The right leg was shortened and externally rotated. Arrival INR was 2.6. Warfarin was held on arrival.",
      "provenance": "cliniproof_episode_generated"
    },
    {
      "note_id": "NOTE-G2-019-002",
      "case_id": "G2-019",
      "note_type": "hospital_course",
      "note_text": "A radiograph, not the history alone, showed a right femoral-neck fracture. The INR was repeated before the operating room. The fracture diagnosis followed the radiograph. Anticoagulation management was then planned around that injury. Surgery waited until the INR was 1.3. No separate reversal drug was charted. Open reduction and internal fixation was then performed. A wound hematoma was present on postoperative day 1 and was not expanding. Hemoglobin was stable. Enoxaparin 40 MG subcutaneously once daily was used for postoperative prophylaxis while warfarin remained held. INR fell from 2.6 to 1.3. Creatinine stayed near 1.2 mg/dL. Pain was controlled with non-opioid measures and the patient could sit and transfer with therapy. Therapy recommended home. The wound assessment is the one above. Warfarin and the prophylactic anticoagulant do not yet have a signed discharge order. Orthopedics, the anticoagulation indication's clinician, and a wound check were arranged.",
      "provenance": "cliniproof_episode_generated"
    }
  ],
